00:05:53.076 --> 00:05:55.245
At a high level, so
what is OpenCensus?

00:05:55.245 --> 00:05:56.120
What is this project?

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:59.030
MORGAN MCLEAN: Yes OpenCensus
is a single distribution

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:00.900
of language specific
libraries that

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:03.401
pull data, so instrumentation
and telemetry,

00:06:03.401 --> 00:06:05.150
out of your application
so you can analyze

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:06.510
the app's performance later on.

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:09.350
So to start with, we're
focusing on distributive traces

00:06:09.350 --> 00:06:10.180
and metrics.

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:11.680
So to put that even
more succinctly,

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:13.190
OpenCensus gives you
a really easy way

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:14.690
to pull traces and
metrics out of your app.

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:16.325
MELANIE WARRICK: Who would be
somebody that would use this?

00:06:16.325 --> 00:06:17.960
MORGAN MCLEAN:
Primarily developers

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:19.630
of distributive services.

00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:23.230
So OpenCensus stems from
a lot of our learnings

00:06:23.230 --> 00:06:25.010
at Google, when
we wanted to build

00:06:25.010 --> 00:06:27.530
distributed tracing,
distributed metrics in a big way

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:28.340
at high scale.

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:30.570
But really sort of the
benefits of OpenCensus

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:32.900
are applicable to
anybody building services

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:34.600
with some amount of scale.

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:37.970
MARK MANDEL: So you two terms
there-- metrics, tracing.

00:06:37.970 --> 00:06:38.930
What are those things?

00:06:38.930 --> 00:06:39.590
What's metrics?

00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:40.250
What's tracing?

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:41.605
What do those two words mean?

00:06:41.605 --> 00:06:43.521
MORGAN MCLEAN: So I
imagine a lot of listeners

00:06:43.521 --> 00:06:45.350
the podcast are
familiar with metrics.

00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:47.480
So traditionally
you'll pull things

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:49.430
like CPU consumption
or memory consumption

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:52.350
out of any of the nodes that
are hosting your application.

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:55.140
And that's, you know, a
fairly well solved problem.

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:57.050
OpenCensus focuses on
applicable metrics,

00:06:57.050 --> 00:06:59.660
so pulling out things like
the number of requests

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:02.109
made to your application or
the latency of those requests,

00:07:02.109 --> 00:07:03.650
as well as any other
metrics that you

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:04.960
want to build into
your code in a custom

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:06.590
way, which is a little
more difficult to pull out

00:07:06.590 --> 00:07:07.130
these days.

00:07:07.130 --> 00:07:08.630
The other term,
distributed tracing,

00:07:08.630 --> 00:07:11.030
is probably one that people
are less familiar with.

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:13.250
So for those who don't
know, a distributed trace

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:16.404
lets you watch a single
request as it propagates

00:07:16.404 --> 00:07:17.570
across all of your services.

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:20.180
So for example, if you have
an e-commerce application

00:07:20.180 --> 00:07:23.570
and a customer comes in and
requests the cart service

00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:25.549
to add an item to
their shopping cart,

00:07:25.549 --> 00:07:27.590
you might get calls to
that shopping cart service

00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:30.454
to then propagate down to maybe
a catalog service and others.

00:07:30.454 --> 00:07:32.870
And a single distributed trace
shows you that entire chain

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:35.390
of requests that was based
off the single action made

00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:36.540
by the customer.

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:37.950
And so it's really useful
for a couple reasons.

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:40.010
First, to determine how your
system operates if you're not

00:07:40.010 --> 00:07:41.250
already familiar with it.

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:42.740
And that's at a
very basic level.

00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:45.099
But additionally, each
hop along the way,

00:07:45.099 --> 00:07:46.140
it shows you the latency.

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:47.980
And so when you want
to reduce the latency

00:07:47.980 --> 00:07:50.354
of your application, which
becomes really important as it

00:07:50.354 --> 00:07:52.563
grows, distributed tracing
is a great way to do that.

00:07:52.563 --> 00:07:54.812
And finally, if you have an
error during that request,

00:07:54.812 --> 00:07:57.770
the trace effectively works like
a really long and powerful call

00:07:57.770 --> 00:07:58.430
stack.

00:07:58.430 --> 00:08:00.712
So you can actually see
what happened that lead

00:08:00.712 --> 00:08:01.449
to that error.

00:08:01.449 --> 00:08:02.990
MELANIE WARRICK:
And my understanding

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:05.390
is that OpenCensus
is Cloud agnostic.

00:08:05.390 --> 00:08:07.647
This is used for any kind
of distributed system.

00:08:07.647 --> 00:08:08.230
Is that right?

00:08:08.230 --> 00:08:08.800
JBD: Yeah.

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:09.620
Correct.

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:12.020
We are not dependent
on any Cloud

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:16.010
provider or metric collection
or tracing backend.

00:08:16.010 --> 00:08:20.165
We collect data from your app
or your services or frameworks

00:08:20.165 --> 00:08:22.520
in a vendor agnostic way.

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:25.580
And then export them
to specific backends.

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:29.720
So it can run anywhere and
against any of the supported

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:30.500
backends.

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:32.583
MARK MANDEL: What sort of
backends do you support?

00:08:32.583 --> 00:08:35.929
JBD: Currently, the support
is dependent on the language

00:08:35.929 --> 00:08:39.000
because lots of exporters
are a work in progress.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:42.169
[INAUDIBLE] we support
to Zipkin, Stackdriver,

00:08:42.169 --> 00:08:46.880
and [INAUDIBLE] for tracing,
Prometheus and Stackdriver

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:48.470
monitoring for
metric collection.

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:49.760
MELANIE WARRICK: Can you
also tell us a little more

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:50.810
about the work that you do?

00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:51.090
JBD: Yeah.

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:53.030
I was actually about
to like it a little bit

00:08:53.030 --> 00:08:55.640
of the question of
what do we do in terms

00:08:55.640 --> 00:08:57.290
of like metric collection.

00:08:57.290 --> 00:09:00.990
As Morgan mentioned that we
are a general purpose library.

00:09:00.990 --> 00:09:04.940
So you can collect metrics
on the measures you define.

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:06.980
It could be related to
your business cases,

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:09.200
such as the number
of transactions

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:14.390
if you're a payment provider
or the number of videos

00:09:14.390 --> 00:09:19.550
you have processed if you are
generating GIFs from videos.

00:09:19.550 --> 00:09:24.050
But on top of that, we
have some canonical metrics

00:09:24.050 --> 00:09:27.530
for protocols such as the
HTTP and RPC framework,

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:32.390
such as latency distribution,
or requests, or response size.

00:09:32.390 --> 00:09:35.630
And I think that's where
OpenCensus is becoming more

00:09:35.630 --> 00:09:38.180
powerful because all
these frameworks can

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:40.880
expose these canonical metrics.

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:43.010
And you just plug
them to your exporter

00:09:43.010 --> 00:09:45.410
and get them out of the
box without doing much.

00:09:45.410 --> 00:09:47.609
MARK MANDEL: It sounds
also like a good reason why

00:09:47.609 --> 00:09:49.400
you'd want to use
something like OpenCensus

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:50.840
rather than just
pushing data straight

00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:52.400
to something like
Stackdriver or Prometheus

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:53.358
or something like that.

00:09:53.358 --> 00:09:56.044
It's giving you tools above and
beyond what the backends you

00:09:56.044 --> 00:09:56.960
provide also give you.

00:09:56.960 --> 00:09:59.820
JBD: They're, again, represented
in the backend somehow.

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:02.390
But it gives these
frameworks to actually

00:10:02.390 --> 00:10:04.550
instrument without
depending on any vendor.

00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:06.080
And it's very powerful.

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:09.650
No framework can be open ended
enough to say that I'm just

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:13.670
going to be using the Prometheus
instrumentation library

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:16.370
and this is the only exposition
format I will support.

00:10:16.370 --> 00:10:19.390
Because we widely depend
on different backends.

00:10:19.390 --> 00:10:21.640
And that's where
OpenCensus shines

00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:23.610
because we're vendor agnostic.

00:10:23.610 --> 00:10:24.450
MARK MANDEL: Sweet.

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:26.380
So actually, JBD,
you're probably

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:28.330
a great person to ask for this.

00:10:28.330 --> 00:10:31.900
As a developer, if I'm
sitting down and I'm like,

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:34.570
I want to use OpenCensus
with maybe and HTTP

00:10:34.570 --> 00:10:36.645
or, I'm guessing, gRPC,
how does that look?

00:10:36.645 --> 00:10:38.020
What does that
process look like?

00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:38.540
JBD: Yeah.

00:10:38.540 --> 00:10:40.960
We currently support
HTTP and gRPC both.

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:43.990
And we usually expect users
to start with our framework

00:10:43.990 --> 00:10:45.130
integrations.

00:10:45.130 --> 00:10:49.210
You pulled a library and you
pulled an integration actually

00:10:49.210 --> 00:10:53.350
for Java, gRPC Java is coming
with OpenCensus integration

00:10:53.350 --> 00:10:54.350
out of the box.

00:10:54.350 --> 00:10:57.850
So you don't have to do any
additional thing in order

00:10:57.850 --> 00:10:59.380
to turn it on.

00:10:59.380 --> 00:11:01.960
And on top of that, you
enable some exporters

00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:04.540
if you want to export
data to the backend.

00:11:04.540 --> 00:11:07.420
We expect users to start with
this framework integrations.

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:11.112
And on top of that, if they have
any fine grained measurements,

00:11:11.112 --> 00:11:13.570
depending on their technical
requirements or their business

00:11:13.570 --> 00:11:17.920
cases, we expect them to
create custom traces or custom

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:20.560
measures and collect
more metrics.

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:25.120
But we expect people to depend
on the framework integrations.

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:28.360
It gives organizations
such an advantage

00:11:28.360 --> 00:11:32.350
to deploy this widely all
across their stack very easily.

00:11:32.350 --> 00:11:35.350
And it's like one of the key
things that we want to achieve.

00:11:35.350 --> 00:11:37.870
OpenCensus or any
instrumentation layer

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:41.290
is just more useful
if you can deploy it

00:11:41.290 --> 00:11:43.045
all across the stack
without much effort.

00:11:43.045 --> 00:11:44.030
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:11:44.030 --> 00:11:45.730
You mentioned Java and Go.

00:11:45.730 --> 00:11:47.470
Morgan, what languages
is supported?

00:11:47.470 --> 00:11:50.020
MORGAN MCLEAN: Yeah, so right
now we've really good support

00:11:50.020 --> 00:11:54.100
across Java, Go and C++
for both tracing and stats.

00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:56.780
And I believe we have tracing
support for Ruby, Python,

00:11:56.780 --> 00:11:58.700
and PHP on top of that.

00:11:58.700 --> 00:12:01.590
Eventually our goal, and we're
already working towards this,

00:12:01.590 --> 00:12:06.520
is to get tracing and stat
support across Java, Go, C++,

00:12:06.520 --> 00:12:13.430
.NET, Node Js, Ruby, Python,
PHP, and I'm missing one.

00:12:13.430 --> 00:12:14.545
Which one am I missing?

00:12:14.545 --> 00:12:16.230
JBD: Erlang, I'm guessing.

00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:18.180
MORGAN MCLEAN:
Erlang, that's still--

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:20.801
I might still be missing
another one, but yeah.

00:12:20.801 --> 00:12:23.230
MELANIE WARRICK: Since
this is an open project,

00:12:23.230 --> 00:12:27.714
how can people contribute that
are not necessarily at Google?

00:12:27.714 --> 00:12:29.380
Or how can people
contribute in general?

00:12:29.380 --> 00:12:30.880
JBD: It's very straightforward.

00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:34.570
You to our GitHub
organization, pick the language

00:12:34.570 --> 00:12:35.900
implementation you want.

00:12:35.900 --> 00:12:38.806
So we actually have two big--

00:12:38.806 --> 00:12:40.180
we have language
implementations.

00:12:40.180 --> 00:12:42.280
But on top of that, we
also have a specs repo

00:12:42.280 --> 00:12:48.494
where we discuss the concepts
and the overall design issues.

00:12:48.494 --> 00:12:51.160
If you want to contribute to the
design issues and the concepts,

00:12:51.160 --> 00:12:55.360
you go to the specs repo,
either start an issue

00:12:55.360 --> 00:12:59.410
or start a pull request if
it's a trivial change that

00:12:59.410 --> 00:13:03.894
can be handled in the scope
of PR and contribute that way.

00:13:03.894 --> 00:13:06.310
If you want to contribute to
the language implementations,

00:13:06.310 --> 00:13:08.950
you need to go to the
language implementation repo.

00:13:08.950 --> 00:13:12.370
And again, we have
contributing docs.

00:13:12.370 --> 00:13:15.034
So you just follow the
steps and contribute.

00:13:15.034 --> 00:13:17.700
MELANIE WARRICK: Similar to most
open source projects basically.

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:18.895
JBD: Yeah, exactly.

00:13:18.895 --> 00:13:19.900
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:13:19.900 --> 00:13:22.030
It also sounds like
at Google or at least

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:23.800
coming out of Google
in some other ways

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:26.320
we've got a lot of
projects that seem to be

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:27.550
doing related sort of things.

00:13:27.550 --> 00:13:29.020
I'm thinking like we
were talking about gRPC

00:13:29.020 --> 00:13:30.130
just a little bit ago.

00:13:30.130 --> 00:13:31.930
Istio seems to come
to mind as well.

00:13:31.930 --> 00:13:33.557
Is there overlap there?

00:13:33.557 --> 00:13:35.140
I mean, you said you
support the gRPC,

00:13:35.140 --> 00:13:39.040
but is there overlap from
gRPC or Istio with OpenCensus

00:13:39.040 --> 00:13:41.090
or like hooks or anything
special going on there?

00:13:41.090 --> 00:13:42.250
MORGAN MCLEAN: Yeah.

00:13:42.250 --> 00:13:43.945
It's kind of
interesting, because just

00:13:43.945 --> 00:13:45.820
to give you a bit of
history, like OpenCensus

00:13:45.820 --> 00:13:47.850
is an open source version of
something called Census which

00:13:47.850 --> 00:13:50.710
is our instrumentation framework
that we use inside of Google.

00:13:50.710 --> 00:13:54.640
And in many ways, gRPC and Envoy
are equivalents of the systems

00:13:54.640 --> 00:13:57.050
that Census inside
of Google hooks into.

00:13:57.050 --> 00:13:59.200
And so interestingly,
with these other projects

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:00.820
being open source
at the same time,

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:02.675
we have a big opportunity
to integrate with them,

00:14:02.675 --> 00:14:04.630
to give external developers
the same experience

00:14:04.630 --> 00:14:06.963
we have inside of Google for
basically automatic tracing

00:14:06.963 --> 00:14:09.370
on all requests that
propagate through our network.

00:14:09.370 --> 00:14:12.280
And so to answer your question
a little more succinctly

00:14:12.280 --> 00:14:13.720
then, yes.

00:14:13.720 --> 00:14:17.200
So we've already built
automatic integration for gRPC.

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:19.810
We're building automatic
integration for Envoy

00:14:19.810 --> 00:14:20.655
right now.

00:14:20.655 --> 00:14:23.920
And so Envoy, of course, is the
application side car component

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:25.450
of the Istio project.

00:14:25.450 --> 00:14:27.430
And then for the
Istio Mixer, which

00:14:27.430 --> 00:14:29.339
is another part of
the Istio project,

00:14:29.339 --> 00:14:30.880
we have some designs
that we'll start

00:14:30.880 --> 00:14:32.680
working on shortly
for doing things

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:33.940
like tracing aggregation.

00:14:33.940 --> 00:14:36.475
So you can capture traces
and configure your backend

00:14:36.475 --> 00:14:37.690
a little more efficiently.

00:14:37.690 --> 00:14:38.710
MELANIE WARRICK:
Morgan, you were talking

00:14:38.710 --> 00:14:40.001
a little bit about the history.

00:14:40.001 --> 00:14:42.227
But did it go from being
Census to OpenCensus?

00:14:42.227 --> 00:14:43.060
MORGAN MCLEAN: Yeah.

00:14:43.060 --> 00:14:44.200
It's actually a
really good question.

00:14:44.200 --> 00:14:46.033
We have a blog post
coming out on this soon.

00:14:46.033 --> 00:14:48.970
So there were a couple
of factors at play.

00:14:48.970 --> 00:14:50.772
For one, the census
team had simply

00:14:50.772 --> 00:14:53.230
been wanting to open source
their work for quite some time.

00:14:53.230 --> 00:14:55.842
And Google has a long history
of open sourcing projects

00:14:55.842 --> 00:14:58.300
even when there's no sort of
ulterior motivate to doing it,

00:14:58.300 --> 00:14:59.950
just because teams
inside are encouraged

00:14:59.950 --> 00:15:02.200
to do that and make things
available to the community.

00:15:02.200 --> 00:15:04.330
Next, on Stackdriver
trace, we were

00:15:04.330 --> 00:15:06.770
developing our own
instrumentation libraries.

00:15:06.770 --> 00:15:08.460
So this effort in
the long run is going

00:15:08.460 --> 00:15:10.240
to be replaced with OpenCensus.

00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:12.170
And the cost of developing
these libraries,

00:15:12.170 --> 00:15:15.190
as any vendor in the industry
can attest to, is rather high.

00:15:15.190 --> 00:15:18.000
And on top of that, the cost
of maintaining these libraries,

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:21.060
because you have to have these
integrations into various web

00:15:21.060 --> 00:15:23.270
frameworks across so
many languages, just

00:15:23.270 --> 00:15:24.390
continues to be high.

00:15:24.390 --> 00:15:26.380
And so you have these
very large teams

00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:29.400
who are just ensuring that
your tracing system can work

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:32.610
with framework x on language y.

00:15:32.610 --> 00:15:35.280
And so we were hoping to
find a more creative solution

00:15:35.280 --> 00:15:37.530
to addressing that, both
for us and our competitors.

00:15:37.530 --> 00:15:39.360
And then finally,
at Google, there

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:41.640
was a need particularly
on the storage teams,

00:15:41.640 --> 00:15:44.880
to get more instrumentation
into Cloud client libraries, so

00:15:44.880 --> 00:15:47.730
particularly services like
Big Table and Spanner.

00:15:47.730 --> 00:15:50.114
Customers do sometimes have
performance challenges.

00:15:50.114 --> 00:15:51.780
And we need a quicker
way of determining

00:15:51.780 --> 00:15:54.190
if that's due to something
happening on the customer's

00:15:54.190 --> 00:15:56.150
end, maybe they are
calling an API in a way

00:15:56.150 --> 00:15:56.940
that we don't'
really recommended,

00:15:56.940 --> 00:15:58.519
a way it's not designed for.

00:15:58.519 --> 00:16:00.810
Or maybe there's legitimately
a problem on the service.

00:16:00.810 --> 00:16:03.520
And those teams in the past
have lacked a lot of insight

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:04.020
into that.

00:16:04.020 --> 00:16:06.180
So resolving some customer
performance issues

00:16:06.180 --> 00:16:08.980
would take days, or
even just more hours

00:16:08.980 --> 00:16:10.390
than they would want.

00:16:10.390 --> 00:16:15.330
And so, getting a way for them
to get both trace requests

00:16:15.330 --> 00:16:18.180
going through from their
library storage services

00:16:18.180 --> 00:16:21.290
and also just ensure that they
can give more instrumentation

00:16:21.290 --> 00:16:23.582
to customers, regardless of
whatever tracing or metrics

00:16:23.582 --> 00:16:24.706
backend that they're using.

00:16:24.706 --> 00:16:25.800
So not just Stackdriver.

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:29.690
And so really, the decision
to release and so heavily fund

00:16:29.690 --> 00:16:32.070
the OpenCensus project
was due to a combination

00:16:32.070 --> 00:16:32.969
of these factors.

00:16:32.969 --> 00:16:34.510
And it was sort of
this perfect storm

00:16:34.510 --> 00:16:36.332
of wanting to open
source things,

00:16:36.332 --> 00:16:38.790
that our own tracing product
needed better instrumentation,

00:16:38.790 --> 00:16:40.800
and then teams at Google
wanted their customers

00:16:40.800 --> 00:16:42.900
to gain much more insight
into their client libraries.

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:44.230
And it worked out really well.

00:16:44.230 --> 00:16:44.670
MELANIE WARRICK:
I could definitely

00:16:44.670 --> 00:16:46.930
see the value of having
that holistic picture

00:16:46.930 --> 00:16:49.500
and being able to sort of
move from being specifically

00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:51.570
affiliated to a certain
product or service

00:16:51.570 --> 00:16:54.457
because that way you can drive
after that holistic tracing.

00:16:54.457 --> 00:16:55.290
MORGAN MCLEAN: Yeah.

00:16:55.290 --> 00:16:55.789
Yeah.

00:16:55.789 --> 00:16:58.245
It would be a much more
challenging conversation,

00:16:58.245 --> 00:17:00.610
with our partners in particular,
if we just went to them

00:17:00.610 --> 00:17:02.460
and we're like, yeah,
this is the library's

00:17:02.460 --> 00:17:05.420
recruited for our own
Stackdriver tracing products.

00:17:05.420 --> 00:17:06.569
You should use them too.

00:17:06.569 --> 00:17:08.527
A lot of partners wouldn't
take that seriously,

00:17:08.527 --> 00:17:09.627
for very good reasons.

00:17:09.627 --> 00:17:11.460
And they would be
somewhat suspicious of it.

00:17:11.460 --> 00:17:13.109
But it's interesting,
when we meet

00:17:13.109 --> 00:17:15.986
with other vendors, particularly
other vendors in this space who

00:17:15.986 --> 00:17:18.145
are also Cloud vendors
like ourselves or at least

00:17:18.145 --> 00:17:21.361
own large, well used APIs, they
have the exact same challenges.

00:17:21.361 --> 00:17:23.569
And so it becomes a very
straightforward conversation

00:17:23.569 --> 00:17:25.380
because we say, hey you can
embed this in your client

00:17:25.380 --> 00:17:26.460
libraries as well.

00:17:26.460 --> 00:17:27.680
And it turns out--

00:17:27.680 --> 00:17:29.430
I suspect, I don't
know this for certain--

00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:31.676
but I suspect that the
other Cloud vendors,

00:17:31.676 --> 00:17:33.300
they also want this
to work when people

00:17:33.300 --> 00:17:35.760
are using third party tracing
applications, not just

00:17:35.760 --> 00:17:36.270
their own.

00:17:36.270 --> 00:17:38.340
And so the fact that
this is an open project

00:17:38.340 --> 00:17:40.500
and it's going to be gaining a
lot more partners in the future

00:17:40.500 --> 00:17:41.250
is very appealing.

00:17:41.250 --> 00:17:43.680
JBD: As a maintainer of some
of these client libraries,

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:47.370
we finally realized that there
is no point of having no vendor

00:17:47.370 --> 00:17:49.140
agnostic solution.

00:17:49.140 --> 00:17:51.769
And I think within the Go
programming language scope,

00:17:51.769 --> 00:17:53.310
we were actually
thinking about maybe

00:17:53.310 --> 00:17:55.771
we should have some
tracing and some sort

00:17:55.771 --> 00:17:57.270
of like instrumentation
library, not

00:17:57.270 --> 00:17:59.980
just tracing but metrics
in the standard library.

00:17:59.980 --> 00:18:02.850
But it became quite a clear
case that this is not just

00:18:02.850 --> 00:18:04.110
like one language.

00:18:04.110 --> 00:18:06.570
Any organization you go,
they depend on three or four

00:18:06.570 --> 00:18:07.110
languages.

00:18:07.110 --> 00:18:09.510
So you need to be
able to provide this

00:18:09.510 --> 00:18:10.950
all across different languages.

00:18:10.950 --> 00:18:14.850
And then I saw the actual,
clear benefit of OpenCensus

00:18:14.850 --> 00:18:18.420
and just decided to join this
project rather than doing

00:18:18.420 --> 00:18:20.265
things in my own corner.

00:18:20.265 --> 00:18:21.150
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:18:21.150 --> 00:18:23.370
I mean, it sounds actually like
that the whole vision of it

00:18:23.370 --> 00:18:24.030
sounds amazing.

00:18:24.030 --> 00:18:26.870
And in theory, if
I set OpenCensus

00:18:26.870 --> 00:18:29.677
inside my application, I
could have metrics and tracing

00:18:29.677 --> 00:18:32.010
that goes all the way through
from my custom application

00:18:32.010 --> 00:18:33.690
code all the way
through to the SDKs

00:18:33.690 --> 00:18:36.140
that I use to integrate
with the Cloud that I'm on.

00:18:36.140 --> 00:18:38.550
And then potentially other
Clouds or other places

00:18:38.550 --> 00:18:39.120
around, assuming they also--

00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:39.920
MORGAN MCLEAN: Yep.

00:18:39.920 --> 00:18:41.260
Or other APIs that
you're calling.

00:18:41.260 --> 00:18:41.470
Yeah.

00:18:41.470 --> 00:18:43.170
MARK MANDEL: That
sounds kind of awesome.

00:18:43.170 --> 00:18:44.753
MELANIE WARRICK:
Yeah, really it does.

00:18:44.753 --> 00:18:47.380
JBD: And there's also an
additional [INAUDIBLE] format

00:18:47.380 --> 00:18:48.900
standardization effort.

00:18:48.900 --> 00:18:51.450
So lots of vendors want
to also standardize

00:18:51.450 --> 00:18:54.190
how we propagate the
trace IDs around.

00:18:54.190 --> 00:18:57.780
So if a trace has, for
example, been started somewhere

00:18:57.780 --> 00:19:01.560
in AWS X-Ray, we will be able
to understand it and like

00:19:01.560 --> 00:19:04.600
keep adding more to
that trace and so on.

00:19:04.600 --> 00:19:07.440
So there's a big effort
all across other vendors

00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:11.500
to finally create this agnostic
view so we can actually

00:19:11.500 --> 00:19:15.434
gather the instrumentation
data and utilize it.

00:19:15.434 --> 00:19:17.100
MELANIE WARRICK: What
additional feature

00:19:17.100 --> 00:19:19.520
enhancements or connections
are you working on?

00:19:19.520 --> 00:19:20.722
MORGAN MCLEAN: Yeah.

00:19:20.722 --> 00:19:21.930
We've got a couple of things.

00:19:21.930 --> 00:19:23.346
And we looked sort
of longer term,

00:19:23.346 --> 00:19:26.070
sort of through the end
of 2018 for OpenCensus.

00:19:26.070 --> 00:19:27.755
One is something
that we've called,

00:19:27.755 --> 00:19:29.130
it's got several
different names,

00:19:29.130 --> 00:19:33.230
but it's sort of retroactive
tracing or delayed sampling.

00:19:33.230 --> 00:19:35.370
But effectively, it's
making a decision

00:19:35.370 --> 00:19:38.025
on whether you want to trace a
request, not at the beginning,

00:19:38.025 --> 00:19:40.150
so not when the request
comes in and hits your load

00:19:40.150 --> 00:19:43.120
balancer or your very first
service, but later on.

00:19:43.120 --> 00:19:45.810
And what this would
enable is selectively

00:19:45.810 --> 00:19:48.360
sampling requests that
had errors, for example,

00:19:48.360 --> 00:19:50.290
or requests that
have higher latency.

00:19:50.290 --> 00:19:52.220
This is not a trivial
things to achieve.

00:19:52.220 --> 00:19:53.370
But we think we can do it.

00:19:53.370 --> 00:19:55.260
And we want to add it to
OpenCensus for all vendors

00:19:55.260 --> 00:19:56.370
to take advantage of.

00:19:56.370 --> 00:19:59.320
If I look more at sort of
the breadth for OpenCensus,

00:19:59.320 --> 00:20:02.820
there's a couple sort of big
rocks that we want to focus on.

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:05.400
The biggest would
be errors and logs.

00:20:05.400 --> 00:20:08.190
And so we haven't put a ton
of thought into this yet.

00:20:08.190 --> 00:20:10.630
But one of the
biggest advantages

00:20:10.630 --> 00:20:13.260
that we see in our telemetry
systems internally inside

00:20:13.260 --> 00:20:17.410
of Google is being able to
link traces to logs or traces

00:20:17.410 --> 00:20:19.130
to other signals,
basically being

00:20:19.130 --> 00:20:20.942
able to take the
context of a request

00:20:20.942 --> 00:20:22.650
where something happened
and include that

00:20:22.650 --> 00:20:25.070
on any other signals
associated with that request.

00:20:25.070 --> 00:20:27.740
And we've also seen that being
very effective with Stackdriver

00:20:27.740 --> 00:20:29.260
trace, our customers
on Google App

00:20:29.260 --> 00:20:30.920
Engine who get this built in.

00:20:30.920 --> 00:20:35.086
And it's sort of annoyingly
hard to pull this off today

00:20:35.086 --> 00:20:37.460
when you have like separate
tracing and logging and error

00:20:37.460 --> 00:20:40.700
reporting and profiling
and other libraries.

00:20:40.700 --> 00:20:42.810
Because most of them
don't look for each other.

00:20:42.810 --> 00:20:44.976
You might be using libraries
from different vendors.

00:20:44.976 --> 00:20:46.819
But if we bring this
in through OpenCensus

00:20:46.819 --> 00:20:48.485
and it actually starts
gaining traction,

00:20:48.485 --> 00:20:50.570
and achieves some
amount of popularity,

00:20:50.570 --> 00:20:52.010
everyone can
benefit, even vendors

00:20:52.010 --> 00:20:54.020
who only offer a single
feature like maybe

00:20:54.020 --> 00:20:58.110
logging or only tracing can
benefit from that correlation

00:20:58.110 --> 00:20:58.610
signals.

00:20:58.610 --> 00:21:00.360
And their customers
can benefit from being

00:21:00.360 --> 00:21:03.140
able to go from a trace
that might be in Zipkin

00:21:03.140 --> 00:21:05.650
and tie that in to some
metrics that are in Prometheus

00:21:05.650 --> 00:21:07.990
or log stored somewhere else.

00:21:07.990 --> 00:21:10.414
And that's really like one of
the big, big things that we

00:21:10.414 --> 00:21:12.080
want to drive long
term with Open Census

00:21:12.080 --> 00:21:13.860
is that correlation
between signals.

00:21:13.860 --> 00:21:15.500
MARK MANDEL: That was
going to lead into question

00:21:15.500 --> 00:21:17.291
I was thinking about
as you were saying it,

00:21:17.291 --> 00:21:19.220
which is you do both
tracing and metrics.

00:21:19.220 --> 00:21:21.230
Like why do you put those
two things together?

00:21:21.230 --> 00:21:23.330
MORGAN MCLEAN: Part of
it's just that those

00:21:23.330 --> 00:21:25.520
are sort of two areas
in the market where

00:21:25.520 --> 00:21:28.760
we saw a need for some amount
of-- perhaps standardization

00:21:28.760 --> 00:21:32.090
is the wrong word, but some
amount of convergence, where

00:21:32.090 --> 00:21:34.700
the market for a long time
hadn't been that well-served

00:21:34.700 --> 00:21:36.860
by projects that did that.

00:21:36.860 --> 00:21:40.160
But additionally, it's that
our visions are much grander.

00:21:40.160 --> 00:21:41.940
Tracing and metrics
is just a start.

00:21:41.940 --> 00:21:44.600
We eventually want to
bring in logs, errors,

00:21:44.600 --> 00:21:47.420
profiling software, any other
signals that we can bring in

00:21:47.420 --> 00:21:50.240
JBD: I want to say that
OpenCensus is a cross tech

00:21:50.240 --> 00:21:51.990
instrumentation framework.

00:21:51.990 --> 00:21:54.380
What we mean by cross
tech is we actually

00:21:54.380 --> 00:21:56.450
propagate some dimensions.

00:21:56.450 --> 00:21:57.830
We call them tags.

00:21:57.830 --> 00:22:00.830
So you can have multiple
different perspectives

00:22:00.830 --> 00:22:03.660
to take a look at your
instrumentation data.

00:22:03.660 --> 00:22:06.000
And these tags are
arbitrary things.

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:08.710
For example, you can ask
questions like, give me

00:22:08.710 --> 00:22:11.150
all the metrics
come in, originated

00:22:11.150 --> 00:22:13.230
at this particular service.

00:22:13.230 --> 00:22:16.250
Or give me all the metrics
that has been originated

00:22:16.250 --> 00:22:18.650
by this specific customer.

00:22:18.650 --> 00:22:23.750
So the goal is actually have
the tag propagation in place.

00:22:23.750 --> 00:22:26.762
And then multiple other signal
types can benefit from that.

