00:26:42.154 --> 00:26:43.820
But it's something I
want to talk about.

00:26:43.820 --> 00:26:46.361
So, yeah, so what does it mean
to natively extend Kubernetes.

00:26:46.361 --> 00:26:49.520
Essentially this means that
Kubernetes has the ability

00:26:49.520 --> 00:26:53.690
to essentially add endpoints
to the underlying API which

00:26:53.690 --> 00:26:54.620
then propagates out.

00:26:54.620 --> 00:26:57.620
So if you want to add your
own nouns to Kubernetes,

00:26:57.620 --> 00:27:00.140
for example, so you know how
we have deployments and pods

00:27:00.140 --> 00:27:01.087
and things like that.

00:27:01.087 --> 00:27:03.170
Kubernetes has these things
called custom resource

00:27:03.170 --> 00:27:05.870
definitions which you
can configure and install

00:27:05.870 --> 00:27:09.140
through YAML files of the
API to add your own nouns.

00:27:09.140 --> 00:27:11.930
So if you want to add an
Acme or a Foo or a widget

00:27:11.930 --> 00:27:14.300
or whatever that thing
is that you can then

00:27:14.300 --> 00:27:16.610
store configuration
information against,

00:27:16.610 --> 00:27:18.170
you can do that
inside Kubernetes.

00:27:18.170 --> 00:27:19.100
I'm going to add
some more links.

00:27:19.100 --> 00:27:21.110
But you can do that through
custom resource definitions.

00:27:21.110 --> 00:27:22.730
And the second part
is a set of things

00:27:22.730 --> 00:27:24.440
that probably either heard
of either controllers

00:27:24.440 --> 00:27:25.190
or operators.

00:27:25.190 --> 00:27:27.660
I tend to use the term
controllers just because that's

00:27:27.660 --> 00:27:28.449
the one I prefer.

00:27:28.449 --> 00:27:30.740
But those are the things that
are custom pieces of code

00:27:30.740 --> 00:27:34.850
you can put inside Kubernetes
to react to when custom resource

00:27:34.850 --> 00:27:37.310
definitions, or other things,
really anything, happens

00:27:37.310 --> 00:27:38.600
inside Kubernetes.

00:27:38.600 --> 00:27:40.910
So you may have seen there's
like a [? Memcached ?]

00:27:40.910 --> 00:27:41.497
operator.

00:27:41.497 --> 00:27:42.830
I've seen [INAUDIBLE] operators.

00:27:42.830 --> 00:27:44.871
I've seen a bunch of
different ones around there.

00:27:44.871 --> 00:27:47.600
Agones itself has custom
resource definitions

00:27:47.600 --> 00:27:49.550
for game servers and
a controller for that.

00:27:49.550 --> 00:27:51.076
But that combination
combined means

00:27:51.076 --> 00:27:53.450
that suddenly you can install
this stuff into Kubernetes,

00:27:53.450 --> 00:27:56.260
I can then go kubectl, get
game server, for example,

00:27:56.260 --> 00:27:59.660
with the Agones and it shows
up natively inside Kubernetes.

00:27:59.660 --> 00:28:01.580
I even got code completion
too, which is cool.

00:28:01.580 --> 00:28:03.260
If I look at the
API points, I can

00:28:03.260 --> 00:28:05.060
see that extra stuff
has been added.

00:28:05.060 --> 00:28:06.930
All that stuff is native
inside Kubernetes.

00:28:06.930 --> 00:28:08.660
Like you can take
advantage of security.

00:28:08.660 --> 00:28:09.830
It's all really,
really cool stuff.

00:28:09.830 --> 00:28:11.210
Stuff I'm really excited about.

00:28:11.210 --> 00:28:12.950
But with those two things,
those are the two key words

00:28:12.950 --> 00:28:14.040
that you're looking for there.

